# GoldClub lab cabinet access (SMB + PsExec)

All GoldClub lab cabinets authenticate with the lab account **`GOLD-CLUB\test`**,
password **`test`** (lab-only credential — never reuse for production).

The interactive workstation user is **not** a local admin on every cabinet
(notably `10.0.0.171`), so without a stored credential, SMB and PsExec return
**"Access is denied" / System error 5**.

## Auto-apply (do this, don't ask the user to enter SMB creds)

SMB share access is made automatic by a **persistent `cmdkey` mapping** (stored in
Windows Credential Manager; survives logoff/reboot). If a `\\<ip>\c$\...` read or
PsExec call fails with access denied, register the cabinet first:

```powershell
cmdkey /add:<ip> /user:GOLD-CLUB\test /pass:test
```

Or register the whole known fleet at once (idempotent):

```powershell
.\Initialize-LabAccess.ps1 -Verify
```

Known cabinet IPs: `10.0.0.83`, `10.0.0.90`, `10.0.0.100`, `10.0.0.110`,
`10.0.0.112`, `10.0.0.171`. The working AFT reference cabinet is `10.0.0.90`
(`GST20664`); the credit-blocked target is `10.0.0.171` (`GST19737`).

## PsExec on non-admin cabinets

The remote scripts run `PsExec -s` relying on the caller's network credentials.
For cabinets where the interactive user is not a local admin (e.g. `10.0.0.171`),
pass explicit auth. Dot-source the shared helper:

```powershell
. "$PSScriptRoot\LabAccess.ps1"
$psAuth = Get-LabPsExecArgs          # @('-u','GOLD-CLUB\test','-p','test')
& $PsExecPath "\\$ip" -accepteula @psAuth -s -n 60 powershell.exe -NoProfile ...
```

PsExec to `10.0.0.171` is slow to connect (~40 s) but works once the credential
is supplied. Verified host responds `GST19737`.

## Files

- `Initialize-LabAccess.ps1` — one-shot fleet credential bootstrap (cmdkey + verify).
- `LabAccess.ps1` — dot-sourceable helper: `Get-LabCredential`, `Get-LabPsExecArgs`,
  `Initialize-LabSmbCredential`.
